  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Harrietta was originally named Gaston, then Harriette, and finally Harrietta, a name created by founder James Ashley combining his father Harry and fiancée Henriette Burt.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        20,
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/harietta-michigan-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village first appeared on the map in 1889 as a stop on the Toledo, Ann Arbor and North Michigan Railway, drawing 700 residents and sawmills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/harietta-michigan-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Springdale post office, established north in 1874, moved to Harrietta in 1890, cementing the town’s status as a permanent postal station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/harietta-michigan-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Abandoned storefronts and historic landmarks still dot the town, reminders of its former prosperity during the lumber boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/harietta-michigan-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "As timber resources dwindled, many residents left, turning the once-thriving lumber town into a shadow of its former self.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
